State lives beside them: `config.json`, `manifest.json` (every photo ever seen, kept or skipped, with hashes), `layout.json` (tile positions), `photos/` (originals), `site/` (output), `backup/` (last 5 states), `sheets/` (review images).

## 1. Configure
```bash
cd ~/gallery && python3 gallery.py config title="<title>" caption="<short caption>" accent="#2f7bd9" handle="<x handle, no @, or empty>" shape=heart theme="<theme>" since=<YYYY-MM-DD>
```
Keys: `title caption accent handle shape shape_name(word used in the intro, default = shape) grid(0=auto, else longest side in cells) url project player(true = X player card, needs X approval) since until`.
**Shapes** (`shape=`): `heart` `circle` `square` `star` `bird` (songbird on a branch) `text:LOVE` or `text:MY/MOM` (`/` = new line; bold letters, needs many photos to be legible) `image:/path/file.png[:invert]` (silhouette of any picture: transparent-PNG alpha, otherwise the subject that differs from the border colour; `:invert` swaps) `poly:/path/pts.json` (JSON `[[x,y],...]`, you can draw any outline yourself) `geojson:/path/file.json[#Exact Name]` (state/country outline: US states `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/PublicaMundi/MappingAPI/master/data/geojson/us-states.json#North Carolina`; a country `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/johan/world.geo.json/master/countries/ITA.geo.json` - code = ISO-3166 alpha-3; download first, `curl -sLO`). For "an animal/object I describe": find a clean high-contrast silhouette (web search for a public-domain/transparent PNG), save it, use `image:`; SVG must be rasterised first (`google-chrome --headless=new --screenshot` or `rsvg-convert`). Always look at the preview before accepting a silhouette.
Grid size is automatic: the smallest grid whose shape holds >=1.2x the photo count. Spare cells show **repeats** of existing photos (so the shape is complete from day one); each new photo replaces a repeat, and when none are left the shape grows outward. Existing tiles never move (except `build --regrid`).

## 2. Collect
`python3 gallery.py fetch ...` downloads originals to `photos/`, hashes them, runs the duplicate check, writes `pending.json` + review images `sheets/pending_NN.jpg` (20 numbered photos each) and `sheets/dupes_NN.jpg`.
- **Folder / chat uploads / Drive / Photos export**: put the files in one folder (`~/gallery/inbox`; save photos the owner sends in chat there; download share links with curl or the connector's download tool; unzip exports) then `fetch --folder ~/gallery/inbox [--since <YYYY-MM-DD> --until <YYYY-MM-DD>]`. Date = EXIF capture date, else file time.
- **X account (public posts)**: `fetch --handle NAME --since <YYYY-MM-DD>`. Uses `https://api.fxtwitter.com/2/profile/NAME/statuses` (cursor paged). It **only works with a browser User-Agent and `Accept: application/json`** (plain curl gets a Cloudflare page) - the script sets both and retries. Keeps only the account's ORIGINAL posts (no replies/reposts), photo media only (no video/GIF). Idempotent: media URLs already in the manifest are skipped; later runs scan back `--days-back 3` days from the newest known photo. If it exits with code 2 (blocked), use your X read tool for the user's posts (look up the user id from the handle; exclude replies+retweets; expansions `attachments.media_keys`; media.fields `url,type`; tweet.fields `created_at,referenced_tweets,in_reply_to_user_id`; max_results 100, page with `pagination_token`), save each raw JSON result to a file and run `fetch --handle NAME --x-json f1.json f2.json`.
- Only public photos the owner posted/gave you. Do not fetch other people's accounts without the owner saying it is theirs.

## 3. Duplicates (strict)
`fetch` already compares every new photo with **all** photos ever seen (kept and skipped) and within the batch: identical file/media URL/sha256 -> silently dropped; perceptual hash (phash AND dhash <= 6) -> `suggest_dup` (number shown in red; defaults to skip); both <= 16 -> listed under `similar`. **Look at `sheets/dupes_NN.jpg`** (left = new, right = existing, with distances) and decide: same scene/moment (burst shots, crops, re-posts, screenshots of the same photo) = skip; clearly different photo = keep. If unsure, skip. Re-run `python3 gallery.py dedupe && python3 gallery.py sheets` if you edit anything.

## 4. Classify by looking
View **every** `sheets/pending_NN.jpg` yourself (use an image-viewing tool; for 200+ photos split sheets among sub-agents). Judge only by what you see, against the owner's theme and exclusions; open the original (`pending.json` -> `file`) for borderline ones. Typical skips: screenshots, memes/graphics, documents/receipts, product shots, duplicates, anything outside the theme or on the exclude list, faces the owner said to leave out. Write `decisions.json` covering every `n`: `"skip"` or a short category label (1-2 words, shown on the card):
```json
{"0":"flowers","1":"skip","2":"beach","3":"skip"}
```
`python3 gallery.py apply decisions.json` (`--dry-run` to preview counts). It backs up state, records every photo in the manifest (skipped ones too, so they never come back), makes thumbs (`site/t`), 1200px previews (`site/hq`), places new tiles, rebuilds atlas, `tiles.json`, `index.html`, `play/index.html`, `card.png`, and runs `verify`. Photo-count text (meta description, intro line, tiles.json, share description) is derived from the real number of kept photos on every build - never edit it by hand. A failure restores the previous state automatically. `python3 gallery.py rollback` restores the last backup and rebuilds.
Change look later: `config ...` then `python3 gallery.py build` (text/colour/card) or `build --regrid` (new shape or grid: reshuffles all tiles).

## 5. Preview locally (always before deploying)
```bash
cd ~/gallery/site && (python3 -m http.server 8000 >/dev/null 2>&1 &) ; cd ..
google-chrome --headless=new --no-sandbox --disable-gpu --hide-scrollbars --force-prefers-reduced-motion --virtual-time-budget=5000 --window-size=1000,900 --screenshot=$HOME/gallery/shot_desktop.png http://localhost:8000/
google-chrome --headless=new --no-sandbox --disable-gpu --hide-scrollbars --force-prefers-reduced-motion --virtual-time-budget=5000 --window-size=390,844 --force-device-scale-factor=2 --screenshot=$HOME/gallery/shot_phone.png http://localhost:8000/
```
(`--force-prefers-reduced-motion` skips the fly-in so the screenshot shows the settled mosaic; `--virtual-time-budget` waits for images.) **View both screenshots and `site/card.png`**: shape recognisable, tiles are real photos (not grey), title/caption not cut off, accent colour right, count correct. Interaction test (optional, `pip install playwright`, use `executable_path='/usr/bin/google-chrome'`): move the mouse over the mosaic -> `#tip` becomes `display:block`; click the tip image -> `#lb` shows. Send the owner the screenshots + card.png.
`python3 gallery.py verify` must print `verify: OK` (counts, files, positions, meta tags, card 1200x630, no unfilled `{{placeholders}}`). Share-preview tags written: title, description, og:/twitter: title/description/image (`/card.png?v=<hash>`, the hash changes whenever the card changes so X/iMessage re-fetch it), `twitter:card=summary_large_image`.

## 6. Deploy (Vercel; free account is enough)
1. `npm i -g vercel` (or `npx vercel`). Check `vercel whoami`.
2. **Login handoff** (owner must sign in once; you cannot do it for them): run `vercel login > ~/gallery/vlogin.log 2>&1 &`, read the log, send the owner the device URL (`https://vercel.com/oauth/device?user_code=XXXX-XXXX`) and ask them to open it and approve; poll the log until it says "signed in". Alternative: owner creates a token at vercel.com/account/tokens and you `export VERCEL_TOKEN=...` (the script passes it). Other hosts: any static host works - upload `site/` and set `config url=https://their-url`.
3. Choose a project name (lowercase, short, e.g. `summer-memories`); `python3 gallery.py config project=NAME`.
4. `python3 gallery.py deploy` (optional `--alias NAME.vercel.app` to pin a specific `*.vercel.app` name). It runs `verify`, links/creates the project in `site/.vercel`, `vercel deploy --prod --yes`, discovers the public URL, writes it to `config url`, rebuilds so og:url/og:image use it, redeploys once if the URL changed, then `live-check`.
5. **Live check** (`python3 gallery.py live-check`, exit 0 = pass; retries ~1 min for propagation): `/` and `/play` return 200 with the right title, og tags and `N photos` text; `/tiles.json` count = N; `card.png?v=<hash>` returns 200 and is byte-identical to local `site/card.png`; `atlas.jpg` identical. Then take a screenshot of the live URL like step 5 and view it. Give the owner the live link.
6. To roll back a bad deploy: `python3 gallery.py rollback && python3 gallery.py deploy`.
Notes: X caches link cards per URL (the `?v=` changes force a refresh; titles/descriptions may lag). `*.vercel.app` pages can be opened by anyone with the link - tell the owner the gallery is public. Vercel CLI flags differ slightly between versions; if `deploy` cannot find the URL, run the printed `vercel` commands by hand in `site/`, then `config url=<public url>`, `build`, redeploy, `live-check`.

## Limits
Photos only (no video/GIF). One square-cell mosaic per site. Very thin shapes/letters need many photos. HEIC needs `pillow-heif`. fxtwitter is a third-party public mirror and may break; the X-tool fallback above is the reliable path.
